  "worked_solution": "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\u27e81,-1,0\u27e9,\\,b=\u27e82,-2,-1\u27e9,\\,c=\u27e80,0,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $\u25b3ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\u2220ABC=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14064104/assets/2c81d48afacc4ac5e81ab501bc713009ea4425451fcf8dbf414672aeadbb1679.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =a-b \\\\  & =\u27e81,-1,0\u27e9-\u27e82,-2,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,1,1\u27e9 \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =\u27e80,0,1\u27e9-\u27e82,-2,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2,2,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{BA}||\\overset{\\to }{BC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(1)={0}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
